The external gameplay rationale for the current nethack key scenario
is a separate issue from the internal logic and real-world analogy of
the game.  Regardless of how the current game structure was arrived
at, people will want it to make some sense.  So, while multiple keys
may be a pain in gameplay, I think that's irrelevant here.

In the real world, too, most average-sized people can kick a door in
or break the lock on a chest, given enough time.  That doesn't stop
such things from having (mostly) individualized locks....

Note that my objections to the lock nonsense would mostly disappear if
the abundant skeleton keys were simply removed from the game.  They
seem superfluous anyway - that would leave illicit lockpicks and
credit cards to do the job from a gameplay point of view, without
begging to explain why so many characters in the dungeon happily carry
legitimate master keys to their own and everyone else's stuff.  It
would also make sense if lawful characters confiscate such items (and
don't use them to open locks).

